@nsobid-EBF7275992BFF941822D5F46E8B89F50

# Pokemon Sword December 22 2017 prototype - Disable NX CPU Profiler

@flag print_values
@flag offset_shift 0x100

// Skips nn::profiler::detail::Initialize in gfl::framework::Application::Application.
//
// This build is compiled with NN_PROFILER_USE_EXTERNAL_PROCESS, so the profiler
// library talks to the devkit's "banana" sysmodule over HIPC instead of driving
// PCCOM in-process (which is what the later March 2018 "sword" build does). That
// sysmodule only exists on a devkit with the NX CPU Profiler tool installed, and
// every step of the handshake is fatal on failure:
//
//   nn::profiler::CreateProfilerByHipc  -> NN_ABORT_UNLESS_RESULT_SUCCESS
//   nn::profiler::LibraryInitialize     -> NN_ABORT_UNLESS_RESULT_SUCCESS
//   IProfiler::GetSystemEvent           -> NN_ABORT_UNLESS_RESULT_SUCCESS
//
// so there is no graceful degradation path to reach - the library cannot be told
// "no profiler here". Under an emulator sm has no "banana" to hand out and the
// process dies during Application construction, before nnMain gets going.
//
// The call site discards the nn::Result (the next instruction is an argument-less
// BL nn::oe::Initialize), and every recording path in the library is gated behind
// GetProfilerStatus() != ProfilerStatus_Offline, whose backing static is already 0
// (Offline). nn::profiler::Finalize() and Cleanup() are both gated the same way, so
// nothing later reaches into the uninitialised subsystem. Removing the call is
// therefore inert apart from leaving the 64 MB profiler buffer that the constructor
// allocated just above unused.

@enabled
0005C960 1F2003D5   // BL nn::profiler::detail::Initialize -> NOP
@stop

// Reverse-engineered against beluga.nss (gflib3 / Jenkins job "orion",
// bld_rev 30f225a1cb089f9b2e2f93b596e7b66a9dca8bf8, build 20171222164419)
// and the nn::profiler library sources.
//
// https://github.com/RAMDRAGONS/switch-patches
